{
  "title": "Replace the sync mechanisms with one clean design (owner approves the design first)",
  "description": "Sync has grown several overlapping mechanisms: recipes and tags stamp change_xid by hand in each writer (tags.rs, api/tags/{create,delete,rename}.rs, api/recipes/delete.rs), while the shopping list uses a stamp_change_xid() trigger; shopping-list sync still accepts the deprecated wall-clock last_sync_at path (see blocked-remove-shopping-sync-last-sync-at); iOS sends whole-row updates with expected_version while web uses per-field PATCH endpoints with no versions; categorizer_version touches every shopping item when the catalog changes so clients resync whole lists; xid cursors break after a dump/restore (p3-sync-cursor-after-restore); and any long write transaction holds every client's cursor back. \
A 2026-10-11 fix made a rejected iOS shopping-list update take the server's copy (server returns the conflicted row), which is a stopgap, not a design: because iOS updates send the whole row, an edit made on the phone while a rejected sync is in flight is dropped too. \
Deliverable 1: a short design doc for the owner covering one change-tracking mechanism for every synced table (recipes, tags, shopping list, meal plan if applicable), the cursor format and how it survives restores, conflict policy (server-wins, last-writer-wins per field, or field-level merge) applied the same way on web and iOS, deletes (soft-delete tombstones), and a migration path for installed iOS builds. \
Deliverable 2, only after the owner approves the design: implement it on server, web and iOS with e2e tests.",
